{"version":2,"text":"Text 1: Questions 7 - 14\n\n7.\n\n8.\n\n9.\n\n10.\n\n11.\n\nThe question of partial protection against H1N1 arose\n\nA\n\nbefore spring 2009\n\nB\n\nduring Spring 2009\n\nC\n\nafter spring 2009\n\nD\n\nduring 2008-09\n\nAccording to Danuta Skowronski\n\nA\n\nthe inactivated influenza vaccine may not be having the desired effects.\n\nB\n\nCanada’s near-real-time sentinel system is unique.\n\nC\n\nthe epidemiological studies were counterproductive\n\nD\n\nthe inactivated influenza vaccine has proven to be ineffective.\n\nThe vaccine achieved higher rates of protection in healthy adults when\n\nA\n\nit was supported by physicians.\n\nB\n\nthe sentinel system was expanded.\n\nC\n\nused in the right season.\n\nD\n\nit was matched with other current strains.\n\nWhich one of the following is closest in meaning to the word prudent in paragraph 3?\n\nA\n\nanxious\n\nB\n\ncautious\n\nC\n\nbusy\n\nD\n\nconfused\n\nThe Canadian sentinel study demonstrated that…..\n\nA\n\nage and geography had no effect on the vaccine’s effectiveness.\n\nB\n\nvaccinations on senior citizens is less effective than on younger people.\n\nC\n\nthe vaccination was no longer effective.\n\nD\n\nthe risk of H1N1 seemed higher among people who received the TIV vaccination.\n\nStudy guide for OET © IRS Group 2018\n\nPage 546","layout":{"version":1,"width":609.0,"height":808.0,"blocks":[{"text":"Text 1: Questions 7 - 14","lines":[{"text":"Text 1: Questions 7 - 14","x":0.1116338275862069,"y":0.06232563242574257,"width":0.23529374876847295,"height":0.017956545792079205,"confidence":1,"words":[{"text":"Text","x":0.1116338275862069,"width":0.04218704926108374,"confidence":1},{"text":"1:","x":0.1583933054187192,"width":0.018932183908045987,"confidence":1},{"text":"Questions","x":0.18651515270935962,"width":0.10402561576354678,"confidence":1},{"text":"7","x":0.2951132315270936,"width":0.011868637110016388,"confidence":1},{"text":"-","x":0.3115542972085386,"width":0.007108374384236419,"confid